'''whister†''' (hwis′tėr), ''v. t.'' [A var. of ''whisper,'' simulating ''whist''<sup>1</sup>.] To whisper; recite in a low voice.
{{smaller block|Then returneth she home unto the sicke party, . . . and ''whistereth'' a certaine odde praier with a Pater Noster into his eare. ''Holland,'' tr. of Camden, II. 147. (''Davies.'')}}
{{smaller block|Oft fine ''whistring'' noise shall bring sweete sleepe to thy sences. ''W. Webbe,'' Eng. Poetry (ed. Arber), p. 75. (''Davies.'')}}

'''whistersnefet†, whistersnivet†,''' ''n.'' [Origin obscure.] A hard blow; a buffet. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|A good ''whistersnefet,'' truelie paied on his eare.
{{right|''Udall,'' tr. of Apophthegms of Erasmus, p. 112.}}}}

'''whistle''' (hwis′l), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''whistled,'' ppr. ''whistling.'' [< ME. ''whistlen, whistelen, whystelen,'' < AS. *''hwistlian'' (as seen in AS. ''hwistlere,'' a piper, whistler) = Icel. ''hvīsla,'' whisper, = Sw. ''hvissla,'' whistle, = Dan. ''hvisle,'' whistle, also hiss; freq. from an imitative base *''hwis:'' see ''whisper.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To utter a kind of musical sound

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|I kept aye between him and her, for fear she had ''whistled.''
{{right|''Scott'', Guy Mannering, xxxiii.}}}}

'''To go whistle''', a milder expression for to go to the deuce, or the like.
{{smaller block|This being done, let the law ''go whistle.''
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 4. 715.}}
Your fame is secure; bid the critics ''go whistle.''
{{right|''Shenstone'', The Poet and the Dun.}}}}

'''To whistle down the wind''', to talk to no purpose; hold an idle or futile argument.—'''To whistle for''', to summon by whistling.—'''To whistle for a wind''', a superstitious practice among old seamen of whistling during a calm to obtain a breeze. Such men will not whistle during a storm.
{{smaller block|“Do you not desire to be free?” “Desire! aye, that I do; but I may ''whistle for that wind'' long enough before it will blow.” {{right|''Johnston'', Chrysal, II. 184. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''Whistling coot''', the American black scoter, ''Œdemia americana.'' [Connecticut.] See cut under ''Œdemia.''—'''Whistling dick.''' (''a'') Same as ''whistling thrush.'' [Local, Eng.] (''b'') An Australian bird, ''Colluricincla'' (or ''Collurocincla'' or ''Collyriocincla'') ''harmonica'', the harmonic thrush of Latham, usually placed in the family ''Laniidæ'', now in the ''Prionopidæ'', or another of this genus, as the Tasmanian ''C. rectirostris'' (''C. selbyi''). The species named are 9½ to 10 inches long, chiefly of a gray color varied with brown and white.—'''Whistling duck.''' (''a'') The whistler or widgeon, a duck. (''b'') Same as ''whistlewing.'' (''c'') Same as ''whistling coot.''—'''Whistling eagle, whistling hawk''', ''Haliastur sphenurus'' (one of whose former names was ''Haliaëtus canorus'', of Vigors and Horsfield, 1826), a small eagle or large hawk, 22 inches long, inhabiting the whole of Australia and New Caledonia. It is a congener of the wide-spread Pondicherry eagle, ''H. indus.''—'''Whist-'''

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|'''ling marmot''', the hoary marmot. See cut under ''whistler'', 1 (''c'').—'''Whistling plover.''' See ''plover''.—'''Whistling râle''', sibilant râle. See ''dry râle'', under ''râle''.—'''Whistling snipe.''' (''a'') Same as ''greenshank''. (''b'') See ''snipe''<sup>1</sup>, 1 (''c'').—'''Whistling swan.''' (''a'') The hooper, elk, or whooping swan. See ''swan''<sup>1</sup>, 1. (''b'') In the United States, the common American swan, ''Cygnus americanus'' or ''columbianus'', as distinguished from the trumpeter, ''C. (Olor) buccinator''.—'''Whistling thrush''', the song-thrush, ''Turdus musicus''. See cut under ''thrush''. [Local, Eng.]}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To form, utter, or modulate by whistling: as, to ''whistle'' a tune or air.
{{smaller block|Tunes . . . that he heard the carmen ''whistle''.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iii. 2. 342.}}}}
{{smaller block|I might as well . . . have ''whistled'' jigs to a mile-stone.
{{right|''W. Collins'', Moonstone, xxi.}}}}
'''2.''' To call, direct, or signal by or as by a whistle.
{{smaller block|<poem>He cast off his friends, as a huntsman his pack,
For he knew when he pleased he could ''whistle'' them back.</poem>
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Retaliation.}}}}
{{smaller block|The first blue-bird of spring ''whistled'' them back to the woods. {{right|''Lowell'', Harvard Anniversary.}}}}
'''3†.''' To send with a whistling sound.
{{smaller block|The Spaniards, who lay as yet at a good distance from them behind the Bushes, as secure of their Prey, began to ''whistle'' now and then a shot among them.
{{right|''Dampier'', Voyages, I. 117.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To whistle off''', to send off by a whistle; send from the fist in pursuit of prey: a term in falconry; hence, to dismiss or send away generally; turn loose. Nares remarks, on the quotation from Shakspere, that the hawk seems to have been usually cast off in this way against the wind when sent in pursuit of prey; with it, or down the wind, when turned loose or abandoned.}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>If I do prove her haggard,
Though that her jesses were my dear heart-strings,
I’ld ''whistle'' her ''off'', and let her down the wind,
To prey at fortune.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3. 262.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>This is he,
Left to fill up your triumph; he that basely
''Whistled'' his honour ''off'' to the wind.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Bonduca, iv. 3.}}}}

