'''whistle''' (hwis′l), ''n.'' [< ME. ''whistle'', ''whistel'', ''whystyl'', ''wistle'', < AS. ''hwistle'', a whistle, a pipe: see ''whistle'', ''v.''] '''1.''' A more or less piercing or sharp sound produced by forcing the breath through a small orifice formed by contracting the lips: as, the merry ''whistle'' of a boy.—'''2.''' Any similar sound. {{smaller|Especially—(''a'') The shrill note of a bird.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The great plover’s human ''whistle''.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A sound of this kind produced on an instrument, especially one of the instruments called whistles. See def. 3.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ship-boys . . .
Hear the shrill ''whistle'' which doth order give.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iii., Prol.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Sooner the ''whistle'' of a mariner
Shall sleeke the rough curbs of the ocean back.</poem>
{{right|''Marston'', What You Will, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') A sound made by the wind blowing through branches of trees, the rigging of a vessel, etc., or by a flying missile. (''d'') A call or signal made by whistling.}}
{{smaller block|Such a high calling therefore as this sends not for those drossy spirits that need the lure and ''whistle'' of earthly preferment, like those animals that fetch and carry for a morsell. {{right|''Milton'', On Def. of Humb. Remonst.}}}}
{{smaller block|They [of Scio] have now no domestic partridges that come at a ''whistle'', but great plenty of wild ones of the red sort. {{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, II. ii. 9.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
'''3.''' An instrument or apparatus for producing a whistling sound. {{smaller|Whistles are of various shapes and sizes, but they all utilize the principle of the direct flute or flageolet—that of a stream of air so directed through a tube as to impinge on a sharp edge.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>With ''qwistlis'', & qwes, & other qwaint gere,
Melody of mowthe myrthe for to-here.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6051.}}
A ''whistle'' seems to have been a badge of high command in the navy in the sixteenth century. One is mentioned in the will of Sir Edward Howard (1512) as hung from a rich chain.
{{right|''Fairholt.''}}}}
Specifically—(''a'') The small pipe used in signaling, etc.,
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Boatswains’ Whistle.}}}}
by boatswains, huntsmen, policemen, etc. (''b'') A small tin or wooden tube, fitted with a mouthpiece and pierced generally with six holes, used as a musical toy. Often called a ''penny whistle.'' See ''flageolet.'' (''c'') An instrument sounded by escaping steam, used for giving signals, alarms, etc., on railway-engines, steamships, etc. See cuts under ''steam-whistle'' and ''passenger-engine.''—'''At one’s whistle''', at one’s call.
{{smaller block|Ready ''at his whistle'' to array themselves round him in arms against the commander in chief.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xiii.}}}}

'''Galton’s whistle''', an instrument for testing the power to hear shrill notes.—'''To pay for one’s whistle''', or '''to pay dear for one’s whistle''', to pay a high price for something one fancies; pay dearly for indulging one’s whim, caprice, fancy, or the like. {{smaller|The allusion is to the story Benjamin Franklin tells (Works, ed. 1836, II. 182) of}}
